age  explore so it says the minds deepest  desire what it is that our intellects  really want the most even in its most  elaborate operations parallels man's  unconscious feeling in the face of this  universe what is the relationship that  the subject has to the environments he  says that it's an insistence on  familiarity and appetite for clarity  understanding the world for a man is  reducing it to the human stamping it  with his seal the truism all thought is  anthropomorphic has no other meaning we  want the world to be a familiar place  comfortable reliable someplace that  actually makes sense to us and he says  if man realized that the universe like  him can love and suffer if the universe  were really like us he would be  reconciled if thought discovered in the  shimmering mirrors of phenomena eternal  relations capable of summing them up and  summing themselves up in a single  principle then would be seen an  intellectual joy of which the myth of  the Blessed would be  ulis imitation we want order we want  things to make sense he calls this a  nostalgia for unity the appetite for the  absolute me says this illustrates the  essential impulse of the human drama we  want things to make sense we want them  to be rational we want there to be  meaning that we can rely on not just  meeting up here at the superficial level  meaning running all the way down to the  very roots of being the problem is  that's not what the world is going to  provide you that's not what you get in  this environment that's not what you'll  ever get he thinks we've always got this  nostalgia for something that we feel is  lost or missing but it was never there  to begin with we never had that and  we're always seeking and we want that  from the world and the world can only  give that to us if in fact the world is  rational but unfortunately the world  although it is rational in parts locally  is not globally rational you might say  well yeah but everything's subject to  natural laws rememb